shortest decimal representation that converts back to the same double,
so \code{0.1} becomes 1/10 rather than the binary approximation
3602879701896397/36028797018963968. This matches what R prints.
\item \code{NA}, \code{NaN} and \code{Inf} are rejected.
}

Set \code{options(z3r.doubles = "error")} to reject non-integer doubles and
require exact strings everywhere.
